## 12. Recommended 18-month implementation plan

### Months 0–3: Design and regulatory mapping

- Select precise countries and customer segments;
- appoint the group Sharia advisory board;
- map regulated activities and certification routes;
- identify licensed partners and compliant investment products;
- conduct household and trust research in each market;
- define AI authority limits and prohibited claims.

### Months 3–6: Build the compliant core

- Separate savings, investment, and coaching modules;
- implement asset screening and purification controls;
- create Arabic and Bahasa interfaces;
- build family permissions and privacy safeguards;
- implement deterministic suitability controls;
- prepare regulatory, Sharia, cybersecurity, and model dossiers.

### Months 6–9: Partner and pilot

- Finalize custody, distribution, and outsourcing agreements;
- obtain required local approvals;
- launch closed pilots in one Gulf hub and Malaysia;
- test disclosures, religious terminology, AI explanations, and support;
- conduct independent Sharia, security, and model audits.

### Months 9–12: Controlled launch

- Release a limited product with simple savings goals and diversified portfolios;
- provide human escalation;
- monitor suitability, withdrawals, complaints, and family-sharing issues;
- publish clear governance and certification information.

### Months 12–18: Scale selectively

- enter Saudi Arabia and Indonesia through dedicated local structures;
- add locally approved assets and family features;
- expand distribution partnerships;
- conduct post-launch Sharia and model reviews;
- proceed to additional Gulf markets only when local governance and support are ready.

## Conclusion

The startup should pursue **consistency in customer protection and technology, but localization in contracts, religious governance, household design, licensing, and trust-building**.

The strongest proposition will not be “European AI adapted for Muslim users.” It will be a locally governed savings and investment service in which:

- Islamic-finance principles determine the product architecture;
- local institutions hold and manage customer assets;
- AI explains and supports rather than claiming unquestionable authority;
- customers control whether and how family members participate;
- religious and regulatory approvals are specific, current, and accurately described;
- each market receives a separate legal, cultural, and Sharia implementation.

This approach is slower than a single cross-border launch but materially reduces licensing, reputational, religious-compliance, suitability, and customer-trust risks while creating a more durable basis for regional scale.
